Abstract

This thesis establishes a rigorous mathematical foundation for the design and optimisation of next‑generation space observatories conceived not as isolated monolithic telescopes, but as distributed, adaptive orbital sensing networks. A unified multi‑objective optimisation framework balances scientific information yield, angular resolution, system cost, and energy expenditure under realistic orbital, communication, and formation‑flying constraints. The core of the framework is a state‑space model of observatory dynamics coupled with Bayesian optimal experimental design, permitting the formal derivation of information‑theoretic performance bounds that explicitly incorporate the dominant metrology noise sources.

Key contributions include: a corrected, three‑dimensional treatment of Earth‑obscuration and solar exclusion zones in orbital coverage theorems; the introduction of a piston‑phase error model that degrades interferometric Fisher information and reveals the required metrology precision for imaging; a refined cost model that captures the super‑linear scaling of formation‑flying infrastructure with baseline length and control accuracy; the reformulation of compressed‑sensing recovery using the physically measured closure phases and squared visibilities rather than unobservable complex visibilities; and a critical assessment of technology readiness levels with a roadmap for bridging the gap between current demonstrations and the proposed architecture.

The final architecture comprises 24 low‑Earth orbit (LEO) nodes and 4 L2 halo orbit nodes forming a tetrahedral interferometer with a maximum baseline of 50 km. This configuration achieves near‑continuous full‑sky coverage, provides an angular resolution of approximately 4 microarcseconds at 1 µm, and requires formation‑flying metrology precision at the nanometre level—a capability already in ground‑based development. Numerical simulations validate the analytical bounds on coverage and information scaling, confirming that the distributed array surpasses monolithic telescopes of equivalent cost within clearly defined technology readiness thresholds.

