1. Introduction

1.1 The Observational Bottleneck
Astronomical progress has always been paced by the removal of the dominant observational limitation. Ground‑based telescopes, even those equipped with extreme adaptive optics, remain shackled by atmospheric turbulence, aerosol scattering, and absorption bands that block entire spectral regions. Space observatories escape these constraints, delivering diffraction‑limited imaging, continuous access to ultraviolet, X‑ray, and far‑infrared windows, and an ultra‑stable thermal environment. Yet the prevailing paradigm treats each space observatory as a singular flagship—brittle, expensive, and slow to evolve.

1.2 From Monoliths to Networks: A Critical History
The idea of distributing apertures in space has a long history marked by ambitious design studies and repeated cancellations. The Space Interferometry Mission (SIM) sought microarcsecond astrometry with a single deployable boom; NASA’s Terrestrial Planet Finder Interferometer (TPF‑I) and ESA’s Darwin aimed for formation‑flying nulling interferometers at the Sun–Earth L2 point; the Stellar Imager Vision Mission proposed a 30‑element Fizeau array. All stumbled over the same hurdle: the need to control optical path differences to a fraction of a wavelength between free‑flying spacecraft. While formation flying has been demonstrated at millimetre levels for radar missions (TanDEM‑X, GRACE‑FO), optical interferometry requires nanometre to picometre precision.

Current concepts—the Large Interferometer For Exoplanets (LIFE), the Black Hole Explorer (BHEX)—are revisiting these challenges. This thesis distinguishes itself by not assuming unproven picometre control. Instead, it derives performance bounds as explicit functions of the r.m.s. piston error $\sigma_{\text{piston}}$, thereby quantifying the metrology precision that must be achieved to make distributed optical interferometry scientifically viable.

1.3 Research Questions and Novel Contributions
The central question is: How should the next generation of orbital observatories be designed to maximise scientific information extraction per unit mass, energy, and cost, under realistic orbital mechanics, communications bandwidth, and detector technology constraints?

The five research questions address (1) orbital configurations with realistic three‑dimensional exclusion zones, (2) information‑theoretic performance degradation due to piston noise, (3) cost‑benefit crossover conditions relative to monolithic telescopes, (4) fundamental resolution and sensitivity limits, and (5) computational image reconstruction from incomplete closure observables. The novel contributions include:

· A unified state‑space model coupling orbital dynamics and interferometric noise;
· Corrected coverage bounds for spherical Earth/Sun exclusion caps;
· An effective Fisher information matrix that decays as $e^{-\sigma_\phi^2}$ under Gaussian piston noise;
· A refined formation‑flying cost model with super‑linear baseline scaling;
· A compressed‑sensing image recovery formulation operating directly on squared visibilities and closure phases;
· A TRL‑mapped architecture and a technology maturation roadmap.

