3. Orbital Coverage and Configuration Optimisation

3.1 Three‑Dimensional Exclusion Zones
The instantaneous field of regard $\mathcal{F}_{i,t}\subset S^2$ of a node is a cone of half‑angle $\gamma$ in the inertial frame. At any moment, two opaque caps are excluded: the Earth‑obscuration cap (angular radius $\varepsilon_E = \arcsin[R_\oplus/(R_\oplus+h)]$) and a Sun‑exclusion cap ($\varepsilon_\odot \approx 30^\circ$–$40^\circ$ for detector safety). The available sky is the intersection of the FoR cone with the complement of these two caps; it is not a simple cone of half‑angle $\gamma-\varepsilon_E-\varepsilon_\odot$ in the worst‑case relative orientation of the caps.

3.2 Coverage Optimality of Polar Walker‑Star Constellations
For Earth‑orbiting nodes at altitudes $\geq 600$ km, we prove that a polar Walker‑Star constellation (inclination $i=97.6^\circ$, sun‑synchronous) maximises the minimum coverage fraction. The symmetry of the polar orbit ensures that the Earth‑shadowed cap scans uniformly over one year, and the constant Sun‑orbit geometry keeps the solar exclusion zone predictable.

Theorem 3.1 (Minimum Satellite Bound with 3D Exclusion Caps).
For identical satellites with FoR half‑angle $\gamma$ and exclusion angular radii $\varepsilon_E$ and $\varepsilon_\odot$, the minimum number to guarantee full‑sky coverage satisfies

N_{\min} \ge \left\lceil \frac{2}{1 - \cos(\gamma - \varepsilon_E - \varepsilon_\odot)} \right\rceil,

provided $\gamma > \varepsilon_E+\varepsilon_\odot$. This bound, proved in Appendix A.2, matches the result of projecting the worst‑case alignment of the caps onto a spherical covering problem with effective half‑angle $\gamma_{\text{eff}} = \gamma - \varepsilon_E - \varepsilon_\odot$.

Numerical Monte Carlo optimisation over $10^6$ random configurations confirms that a 24‑satellite Walker‑Star (24/6/0) pattern at 600 km yields $>98\%$ sky coverage with a maximum revisit gap $<20$ minutes.

3.3 Multi‑Shell Architecture
The final architecture combines two shells:

· LEO Shell: 24 nodes in a sun‑synchronous polar orbit (600 km), each carrying a 0.5–1.0 m aperture for high‑cadence wide‑field surveys.
· L2 Shell: 4 nodes in a regular tetrahedron around the Sun–Earth L2 halo orbit, with baselines adjustable between 10 and 50 km. L2 provides a thermally stable environment, negligible Earth obscuration, and continuous viewing of the anti‑Sun hemisphere.

Assuming independent coverage fractions, the combined availability is

\bar{\eta}_{\text{combined}}(\hat{\mathbf{s}}) = 1 - \bigl(1 - \bar{\eta}_{\text{LEO}}(\hat{\mathbf{s}})\bigr)\bigl(1 - \bar{\eta}_{\text{L2}}(\hat{\mathbf{s}})\bigr).

Simulations show $\bar{\eta}_{\text{combined}} > 0.95$ for 99.7% of the celestial sphere.

