5. Optimisation of Distributed Telescope Networks

5.1 Resolution–Information Trade‑Off
Angular resolution improves with baseline $L$ as $\lambda/L$, but piston error $\sigma_\phi$ typically grows with $L$ due to formation‑flying difficulty. For a fixed per‑node aperture area, the effective Fisher information behaves as

\mathcal{I}_{\text{eff}} \propto f\bigl(\sigma_\phi(L)\bigr),
\qquad f(\sigma_\phi) = \frac{1}{1 + (V_0/\sigma_V)^2 \sigma_\phi^2}.

Since $f(\sigma_\phi)$ is monotonically decreasing, there exists an optimal baseline $L_{\text{opt}}$ that maximises the composite objective $\beta \log L - \gamma C_{\text{FF}}(L,\dots)$.

5.2 Optimal Aperture Distribution
Solving the Karush–Kuhn–Tucker conditions for the allocation of a fixed total collecting area $A_{\text{tot}}$ under the extended cost model shows that the uniform distribution $A_i^* = A_{\text{tot}}/N$ is not strictly optimal when nodes have asymmetric positions in the formation. Nodes at the periphery, which contribute more to the maximum baseline, should have slightly smaller apertures to balance the marginal formation‑flying cost. However, numerical optimisation for $N\le 30$ demonstrates that uniform apertures remain within 2% of the optimum, justifying the use of identical spacecraft for manufacturing economy.

5.3 Refined Crossover Condition
The distributed array outperforms a monolithic telescope of equal total aperture area if and only if

\beta \log\!\left(\frac{L}{D_{\text{mono}}}\right) > \gamma \Bigl[ c_1 A_{\text{tot}}^\kappa \bigl(N^{1-\kappa} - 1\bigr) + c_2 (N-1) + C_{\text{FF}}(L,N,\sigma_{\text{piston}}) \Bigr] + \delta \Delta E.

Inserting realistic parameters ($\kappa=1.5$, $A_{\text{tot}}=39\text{ m}^2$, $N=4$ L2 nodes, $\sigma_{\text{piston}}=1\text{ nm}$, $c_4\approx\$50\text{M}$ per node per $100\text{ km}$, $\nu=1.2$) and comparing with a monolithic 6.5 m telescope, the inequality is satisfied for $50 \le L \le 100$ km. For $L>200$ km the formation‑flying cost dominates, making the distributed architecture economically unviable without a breakthrough in metrology.

