7. Proposed Architecture and Technical Specification

7.1 Layer 1: Orbital Infrastructure
The operational network consists of:

· LEO Constellation: 24 satellites, Walker‑Star (24/6/0), altitude 600 km, inclination $97.6^\circ$, providing all‑sky coverage with a maximum gap under 15 min.
· L2 Formation: 4 spacecraft in a regular tetrahedron of side length 50 km, orbiting the Sun–Earth L2 halo. Station‑keeping requires $\Delta v < 5$ m/s per year.

7.2 Layer 2: Sensor Network and Error Budget
Each L2 node carries a 2 m off‑axis primary mirror ($\sim3.1$ m² collecting area), with laser metrology terminals measuring inter‑node distance to 1 nm (r.m.s.)—the performance demonstrated by LISA Pathfinder for inertial sensing and targeted for LISA. LEO nodes use 0.5–1.0 m apertures for photometric and spectroscopic surveys.

The piston error budget for L2 nodes at $\lambda=1$ µm is:

Component Value (rms)
Laser metrology noise 1.0 nm
Structure jitter (fringe tracker) 0.5 nm
Clock synchronisation 10 nm equivalent
Total (RSS) 1.2 nm

The corresponding phase error is $\sigma_\phi = (4\pi/1\,\text{µm})\times 1.2\,\text{nm} \approx 0.015$ rad, satisfying the $\lambda/10$ coherence criterion.

7.3 Layer 3: Computational Layer
On‑board processing uses radiation‑hardened FPGAs for real‑time fringe tracking and GPUs for sparse image reconstruction. The adaptive greedy scheduler runs with a 60‑second granularity, repointing the array to maximise the instantaneous marginal EIG.

7.4 Technology Readiness Assessment
The critical subsystems are mapped to TRL:

· 2‑m telescope: TRL 6 (JWST).
· Laser metrology at 1 nm: TRL 4 (ground demonstrators; LISA will raise it to 5).
· Formation flying at 50 km: TRL 5 (PRISMA, TanDEM‑X extrapolation).
· Distributed consensus inference: TRL 4 (CubeSat swarm demos).

A dedicated pathfinder mission, deploying two nodes to L2 with the full metrology chain, is the essential next step to raise the metrology TRL to 6 before the full 4‑node array is launched.

