C.3 Derivation of the Refined Crossover Condition (Theorem 5.3)

The composite objective for a monolithic telescope is $J_{\text{mono}} = \alpha I_{\text{mono}} + \beta R_{\text{mono}} - \gamma C_{\text{mono}}$, and for the distributed array $J_{\text{dist}} = \alpha I(N) + \beta R(L) - \gamma C_{\text{dist}} - \delta \Delta E$. To favour distribution, we require $J_{\text{dist}} \ge J_{\text{mono}}$. Assuming $\alpha$ is small or normalized, the dominating terms are the resolution and cost. For the same collecting area, the monolithic telescope has resolution $R_{\text{mono}} = \log(\theta_{\text{ref}} D_{\text{mono}} / \lambda)$ (using $B_{\max}=D_{\text{mono}}$ for a filled aperture), while the distributed array has $R(L) = \log(\theta_{\text{ref}} L / \lambda)$. The difference is $\beta \log(L/D_{\text{mono}})$.

The distributed cost is

C_{\text{dist}} = c_1 A_{\text{tot}}^\kappa N^{1-\kappa} + c_2 N + c_3 M_{\text{launch}} + C_{\text{FF}}(L,N,\sigma_{\text{piston}}).

The monolithic cost for an aperture of area $A_{\text{tot}}$ is $C_{\text{mono}} = c_1 A_{\text{tot}}^\kappa + c_2 + c_3 M_{\text{launch,mono}}$, where we take $N=1$ and no formation‑flying cost.

Subtracting, we obtain

J_{\text{dist}} - J_{\text{mono}} = \beta \log(L/D_{\text{mono}}) - \gamma \bigl[ c_1 A_{\text{tot}}^\kappa (N^{1-\kappa} - 1) + c_2 (N-1) + C_{\text{FF}} \bigr] - \delta \Delta E.

Setting this $\ge 0$ yields the condition in Theorem 5.3. Substituting $C_{\text{FF}} = c_4 N (L/L_0)^\nu (\sigma_{\text{piston}}/\sigma_0)^{-\mu}$ and typical values ($c_1=10^9\$, $\kappa=1.5$, $A_{\text{tot}}=39,\text{m}^2$, $N=4$, $c_2=50\times10^6$, $c_4=50\times10^6\$, $L_0=100,\text{km}$, $\sigma_0=1,\text{nm}$, $\nu=1.2$, $\mu=0.8$, $\sigma_{\text{piston}}=1,\text{nm}$) yields the crossover at $L\approx 75$ km. ∎

